-- REFUSAL FIXTURE (BLAWX-EXPORT-SPEC §8.4, §11 W3; integrated 2026-09-02).
--
-- `l4 blawx` refuses this module; `l4 check` succeeds on it. The refusal is
-- the Blawx leg's, not L4's — the module is perfectly good L4.
--
-- clean-law 0.0.4 (the version Blawx pins) builds its `legal_text` from
-- pyparsing's `printables`, which is ASCII-only. A single character above
-- U+007F anywhere in a section's prose therefore ENDS clean-law's parse of the
-- `rule_text` at that character, and every LATER section's canvas is orphaned:
-- the workspace we name `sec_2_section` belongs to no section of the Act, and
-- nothing in the emitted bytes says so, because both halves are ours and each
-- is self-consistent.
--
-- Measured 2026-09-02 with etc/blawx-eid-harness.py against clean-law 0.0.4 +
-- pyparsing 3.3.2, one character at a time on exactly this module: U+00A3 (£),
-- U+2019 (’), U+00A7 (§) and U+201C (“) each left clean-law yielding
-- ['sec_1_section'] against workspaces ['sec_1_section', 'sec_2_section'].
--
-- `asciiFold` (L4.Blawx.Lower) folds the punctuation legislation actually
-- contains — em/en dashes, curly quotes and apostrophes, the ellipsis, the
-- non-breaking space, the section sign — so a legislation.gov.uk paste keeps
-- working. What is left, such as the currency sign below, is refused by name.

§ `Levy Act`

DECLARE Trader HAS
    pays     IS A BOOLEAN
    resident IS A BOOLEAN

@export A trader who pays the £5 levy is registered.
GIVEN t IS A Trader
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE registered t IF t's pays

@export A resident trader is domiciled here.
GIVEN t IS A Trader
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE domiciled t IF t's resident
